{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794425495,"nonce":"fjp936Tyfgs4sV5PWHLhBAxkMEQ=","title":"The interpreter still keeps some dead values reachable after bd182ac","message":"Left over from bd182ac, which cleared the SSA walker's dead call results and temporaries. Each of these retains a bounded amount, not one object per call site.\n\n1. `ASH_AIR=v2-serialize` (the flat-opcode arm over AIR-optimized bodies) has no clearing. hxn's program still reports 10200 live where 200 is expected. Its registers are the originating HL registers, and the inliner mints a fresh one per inlined call site, so each dead result keeps its own register. A fix is either clearing in that arm's call handling or sharing registers across dead results in the serializer.\n2. A value that dies with no call after it on its path keeps its slot (the clearing is attached to calls).\n3. After `held = null` and several `hl.Gc.major()` calls, hxn's program reports 250 live in interp and hybrid, and 51 under jit. `ASH_AIR=off` gives 250 too, so it is not the SSA walker. `ASH_GC_DEBUG_ROOTS` attributes it to the native thread stack (about 6 KB kept at those collections, about 1 KB once the finalizers have run) and no published frame range marks anything, so a stale word in the interpreter's own Rust frames is the likely holder. Not confirmed.\n\nReproducer for 3: the program in bd182ac with the tail replaced by four `garbage(); hl.Gc.major();` rounds printing `Native.kept_live()` after each.","files":null}]}